yield: for 2kg pulled pork — 6-8 sandwiches
uses:
  - mayo
components:
  - name: cabbage prep
    ingredients:
      - item: green cabbage
        mass: 500
        basis: true
        note: shredded 2-3mm, core removed
      - item: red cabbage
        mass: 150
        note: shredded, for colour
        optional: true
      - item: carrot
        mass: 120
        qty: 1
        unit: ea
        note: large, coarsely grated or julienned
      - item: spring onion
        qty: 3
        unit: ea
        note: thin diagonal slices, whites and greens
      - item: salt
        mass: 10
        note: for pre-salting, mostly rinses away
    steps:
      - shred cabbage 2-3mm — thinner goes limp, thicker won't take dressing
      - toss cabbage with the salt in a colander, sit 30-45 mins over the sink
      - rinse briefly, then squeeze hard in a tea towel until it stops giving water
      - combine with carrot and spring onion
  - name: dressing
    ingredients:
      - item: mayo
        mass: 100
      - item: apple cider vinegar
        mass: 25
      - item: dijon mustard
        mass: 12
      - item: sugar
        mass: 10
      - item: celery seed
        mass: 1
        qty: 0.5
        unit: tsp
        optional: true
      - item: black pepper
        mass: 1
        qty: 0.5
        unit: tsp
      - item: salt
        note: to taste, after dressing — the cabbage is already seasoned
    steps:
      - whisk everything together, taste, adjust vinegar until it's sharper than seems right
  - name: assembly
    steps:
      - dress no more than 30 mins before serving
      - start with two thirds of the dressing, add the rest only if it looks dry
      - taste for salt and vinegar just before it goes on the sandwich
notes: |
  Built to go on a sandwich, so the priority is that it stays crunchy and doesn't leak.
  Pre-salting is the whole trick: it pulls the water out on your terms instead of into the
  bread. Skip it and you get a puddle in the toast within minutes.

  Sharper than a side-dish slaw on purpose — it's cutting rich pork and a vinegar sauce,
  not standing alone. Taste it on its own and it should read slightly too acidic.

  Dress late. Even salted cabbage softens once the dressing is on; an hour ahead is already
  past its best. Undressed prepped veg keeps 2 days in the fridge.

  Vinegar-only version: drop the mayo, go 60g cider vinegar + 40g oil + the sugar and
  mustard. Lighter, holds much longer on the plate, better if the pork is already sauced
  heavily.
